      },
      "source": [
        "### 2.5 Feature Selection Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Removed:** `customer_id` because it is only an identifier.\n",
        "- **Kept for profiling:** `preferred_device` and `total_lifetime_spend_kes`. These will help describe the customer groups after clustering.\n",
        "- **Used for clustering:** `recency_days`, `frequency_per_year`, `avg_order_value_kes` and `num_categories_purchased`.\n",
        "- **Preprocessing note:** `avg_order_value_kes` has 26 missing values (3.47%). These must be handled before scaling."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "N76L6zdT_YDn",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "N76L6zdT_YDn"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 3. Data Preprocessing\n",
        "### Member 3: Preprocessing\n",
        "\n",
        "This step takes the `clustering_features` selected by Member 2 and prepares them for K-Means:\n",
        "handling the missing values identified during EDA, then standardizing every feature so that\n",
        "distance calculations are fair. The output of this section, the fitted `SimpleImputer`, the\n",
        "fitted `StandardScaler`, and the scaled feature matrix `X_scaled` is handed to Member 4 (finding\n",
        "k) and must be kept for Member 6 (model persistence), since any new customer has to be imputed\n",
        "and scaled with these exact fitted objects before a trained K-Means model can assign it to a\n",
        "cluster correctly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "KYHx1u3W_YDn",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "KYHx1u3W_YDn"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 3.1 Why Scaling Is Needed for K-Means\n",
        "\n",
        "K-Means assigns a customer to a cluster by computing the **Euclidean distance** between that\n",
        "customer and each cluster centroid, and it updates centroids by averaging raw feature values.\n",
        "Euclidean distance treats one unit on every axis as equally important, but our four clustering\n",
        "features are not on comparable numeric scales:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Feature                     | Approx. range in this dataset |\n",
        "|------------------------------|-------------------------------|\n",
        "| `recency_days`                | ~1 – 350 (days)                |\n",
        "| `frequency_per_year`          | ~0.2 – 32 (purchases/year)     |\n",
        "| `avg_order_value_kes`         | ~1,000 – 10,000+ (KES)          |\n",
        "| `num_categories_purchased`    | 1 – 9 (categories)             |\n",
        "\n",
        "`avg_order_value_kes` is expressed in thousands of Kenyan shillings while `num_categories_purchased`\n",
        "only ever varies by single digits. If we fed the raw values into K-Means, a difference of, say,\n",
        "2,000 KES in average order value would swamp the squared-distance calculation compared to a\n",
        "difference of even the full 1–9 range in `num_categories_purchased`not because order value is\n",
        "actually a more important behavioral signal, but purely because it is measured in bigger numbers.\n",
        "The clustering result would then mostly reflect spending magnitude and largely ignore recency,\n",
        "frequency, and category breadth.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Standardizing** every feature to mean = 0 and standard deviation = 1 (via `StandardScaler`)\n",
        "removes this artifact of units and range. After scaling, a \"1 standard deviation\" difference means\n",
        "the same thing on every feature, so each of the four behavioral signals gets a comparable say in\n",
        "the distance calculation, and the resulting segments reflect genuine behavioral similarity rather\n",
        "than which feature happened to be measured in the largest numbers."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "pD0aeetM_YDo",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pD0aeetM_YDo"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 3.2 Missing Values: Structural vs. Random, and Why the Distinction Matters\n",
        "\n",
        "Member 1's EDA (Section 1.6) found that only `avg_order_value_kes` has missing values (26 of 750\n",
        "customers, 3.47%). Before choosing how to fill them in, it is important to name *why* they are\n",
        "missing, because the correct treatment depends on it:\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Structural missingness** - the value is missing for a systematic, explainable reason baked\n",
        "  into the data-generating process itself, and the \"missingness\" is itself informative. For\n",
        "  example, if a customer had `frequency_per_year = 0` (never purchased), then `avg_order_value_kes`\n",
        "  would be structurally undefined, there is no order to average, so imputing a typical customer's\n",
        "  order value would fabricate behavior that customer never had. The right treatment for structural\n",
        "  missingness is usually a domain-driven fill (e.g. 0, or a dedicated \"not applicable\" flag), not a\n",
        "  statistical average.\n",
        "- **Random (non-structural) missingness** - the value exists conceptually (the customer *did*\n",
        "  place orders with *some* average value), but it simply was not recorded e.g. a logging gap or\n",
        "  a data-entry error. The missingness carries no behavioral meaning of its own, so it is reasonable\n",
        "  to estimate the missing value from customers with similar, recorded behavior.\n",
        "\n",
        "We re-verify which case applies here before deciding, rather than assuming."
      ]
    },
    {
