        "import joblib\n",
        "import os\n",
        "\n",
        "model_dir = \"../model\"\n",
        "os.makedirs(model_dir, exist_ok=True)\n",
        "\n",
        "preprocessing_path = os.path.join(model_dir, \"ecommerce_preprocessing.joblib\")\n",
        "joblib.dump(\n",
        "    {\n",
        "        \"imputer\": imputer,\n",
        "        \"scaler\": scaler,\n",
        "        \"clustering_features\": clustering_features,\n",
        "    },\n",
        "    preprocessing_path\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\" Fitted imputer and scaler saved to {preprocessing_path}\")\n",
        "print(\"Kept in-memory for the rest of this notebook: `imputer`, `scaler`, `X_imputed`, `X_scaled`, `X_scaled_df`.\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "77vriZ_X_YDq",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "77vriZ_X_YDq"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 3.6 Preprocessing Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Scaling is required** because K-Means clusters by Euclidean distance, and our four clustering\n",
        "  features live on very different numeric scales (days, purchases/year, KES, category counts);\n",
        "  without scaling, `avg_order_value_kes` alone would dominate the distance calculation.\n",
        "- **Missingness was diagnosed, not assumed.** Only `avg_order_value_kes` had missing values\n",
        "  (3.47%), and every affected customer had positive purchase frequency and lifetime spend so the\n",
        "  missingness is **random/non-structural**, not the structural \"customer never purchased\" case,\n",
        "  and was filled with the median.\n",
        "- **The scaled data was verified**, not just visually inspected, to have mean ≈ 0 and standard\n",
        "  deviation ≈ 1 on every feature.\n",
        "- **The fitted `imputer` and `scaler`** were kept as notebook variables and persisted to\n",
        "  `../model/ecommerce_preprocessing.joblib` so Member 6 can apply the identical transformation to\n",
        "  new customers when the final model is deployed.\n",
        "\n",
        "`X_scaled` (a NumPy array) and `X_scaled_df` (the same data as a labeled DataFrame) are now ready\n",
        "for Member 4 to determine the optimal number of clusters."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "92251ab7",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4. Finding Optimal Number of Clusters and Robustness\n",
        "\n",
        "### Member 4: Finding K and Robustness\n",
        "\n",
        "This section determines a suitable number of customer clusters (`k`) using the scaled data prepared by Member 3.\n",
        "\n",
        "Rather than choosing `k` using only one measure, three methods are compared:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Elbow Method (Inertia)** – looks for the point where adding more clusters produces only a small reduction in within-cluster variation.\n",
        "2. **Silhouette Score** – measures how well customers fit within their own cluster compared with other clusters. Higher values are better.\n",
        "3. **Davies-Bouldin Index** – measures similarity between clusters. Lower values indicate more compact and well-separated clusters.\n",
        "\n",
        "We test values of `k` from 2 to 10. After identifying the strongest candidate values, robustness checks are performed to determine whether the clustering conclusion changes when reasonable modelling choices are changed.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "2d8dfeb3",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
