              "    k    Inertia  Silhouette Score  Davies-Bouldin Index\n",
              "0   2  1513.9027            0.4134                0.9736\n",
              "1   3   770.8287            0.5231                0.6316\n",
              "2   4   457.0670            0.5492                0.6718\n",
              "3   5   307.3436            0.5782                0.6775\n",
              "4   6   278.7710            0.5355                0.8564\n",
              "5   7   255.0706            0.4961                0.9797\n",
              "6   8   235.7238            0.4735                1.0746\n",
              "7   9   221.0338            0.4607                1.0728\n",
              "8  10   209.8364            0.4143                1.1169"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 873,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# Test possible numbers of clusters from 2 to 10\n",
        "k_values = range(2, 11)\n",
        "\n",
        "inertias = []\n",
        "silhouette_scores = []\n",
        "davies_bouldin_scores = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for k in k_values:\n",
        "    \n",
        "    # Fit K-Means using the scaled data from Member 3\n",
        "    model = KMeans(\n",
        "        n_clusters=k,\n",
        "        random_state=42,\n",
        "        n_init=20\n",
        "    )\n",
        "    \n",
        "    labels = model.fit_predict(X_scaled)\n",
        "    \n",
        "    # Save the three evaluation measures\n",
        "    inertias.append(model.inertia_)\n",
        "    silhouette_scores.append(silhouette_score(X_scaled, labels))\n",
        "    davies_bouldin_scores.append(davies_bouldin_score(X_scaled, labels))\n",
        "\n",
        "# Put all results into one table\n",
        "k_results = pd.DataFrame({\n",
        "    \"k\": list(k_values),\n",
        "    \"Inertia\": inertias,\n",
        "    \"Silhouette Score\": silhouette_scores,\n",
        "    \"Davies-Bouldin Index\": davies_bouldin_scores\n",
        "})\n",
        "\n",
        "k_results.round(4)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "d8194b00",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The table compares each candidate number of customer segments using three different measures.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **Silhouette Score**, a larger value represents better separation between customer groups.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the **Davies-Bouldin Index**, a smaller value is preferred because it means the customer clusters are more compact and less similar to each other.\n",
        "\n",
        "Inertia always decreases as more clusters are added, so it is interpreted using the elbow plot rather than simply selecting the smallest inertia."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "640c19e4",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### 4.2 Elbow Method"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 874,
      "id": "f3e42d56",
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "image/png": "",
            "text/plain": [
              "<Figure size 800x500 with 1 Axes>"
            ]
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "display_data"
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(\n",
        "    list(k_values),\n",
        "    inertias,\n",
        "    marker=\"o\"\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Number of Clusters (k)\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Inertia\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Elbow Method for E-Commerce Customer Segments\")\n",
        "plt.xticks(list(k_values))\n",
        "plt.grid(alpha=0.3)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "id": "54009257",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        " The elbow method looks for the point where the reduction in inertia begins to slow noticeably. Before the elbow, adding another customer segment substantially improves within-cluster compactness. After the elbow, additional clusters provide progressively smaller improvements.\n",
        "\n",
        "The elbow therefore provides a candidate value of `k`, which will be compared with the Silhouette Score and Davies-Bouldin Index before the final recommendation is passed to Member 5."
      ]
    },
    {
